														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4823546">
									

<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[+]&nbsp;Open&nbsp;for&nbsp;crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>a feral humanoid fish with the traits of multiple types of marine life that has a fear of Doctors.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>What kinda fish traits are we looking at here?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The SCP is one of multiple anomalous entities that were found by Foundation MTF agents that mentioned something about a hospital that hurt them, but after something interfered with the communications between the foundation and the agents, the other entities and MTF agents were taken captive by an unknown threat. the SCP was found by another group of Foundation agents who found it unconscious and injured, along with a note from a Doctor Josef Monstrosity thanking the foundation for finding their med students projects, and left this one as a show of thanks.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'm a little uncertain as to how all this would be shown: like, the first part feels like it'd be vague communications and not really like, something fully in the article? And the second part feels&#8230;fine but like it has weird implications around this doctor that don't really go anywhere?</p>
<hr />
<p>I think, at the end of the day, my biggest concern is that it feels too much like the focus of the narrative is on 'how did we find this thing' when to me the most interesting question is about what happens now it's contained: the fear of doctors and strange fishy nature feels like it'd give a lot of room for exploring how the foundation interacts with this, while the Dr Josef Monstrosity angle feels like it'd give a lot of room for exploring his role in this whole thing and like, what his existence means for the broader world.</p>
<p>It also feels like the narrative is very divorced from the actual scp: like my general rule of thumb is that if you could swap the scp object out for something with completely different properties, then the narrative is probably too broadly focussed.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

